WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.403
This is All in a Row: Compressed Convolution
Networks for Graphs,

00:00:05.483 --> 00:00:09.697
an ICML 2023 paper from the Chinese Academy of Sciences.

00:00:09.777 --> 00:00:15.217
Can we run standard, CNN-style Euclidean
convolution directly on graphs?

00:00:15.297 --> 00:00:20.277
Yes: learn a differentiable permutation that lines
nodes in a row,

00:00:20.357 --> 00:00:22.730
then slide a kernel across them.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.720
The model, CoCN, sets state of the art.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.321
Convolutional graph networks have two weaknesses.

00:00:29.401 --> 00:00:32.689
Because their filters come from graph polynomials,

00:00:32.769 --> 00:00:36.259
they lack expressiveness on a small parameter budget.

00:00:36.339 --> 00:00:40.503
And they cannot learn hierarchical, multi-scale
features alone,

00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:44.275
needing bolt-on tricks like clustering or node dropping.

00:00:44.355 --> 00:00:51.415
Image CNNs have neither problem: local learning and
shared filters naturally capture multi-scale patterns.

00:00:51.495 --> 00:00:55.120
But their Euclidean convolution assumes a regular grid,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.410
and does not fit irregular graphs.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:01.099
So why not borrow Euclidean convolution for graphs?

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:03.891
Convolution is sensitive to spatial order,

00:01:03.971 --> 00:01:07.147
but graph networks must be permutation invariant.

00:01:07.227 --> 00:01:11.933
Earlier methods picked a node ordering
independently of the convolution,

00:01:12.013 --> 00:01:16.253
so it could not be tuned for the task and often
lost information.

00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:21.370
What is missing is an ordering that is
differentiable and learned end to end.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.246
The paper makes two contributions.

00:01:25.326 --> 00:01:28.823
First,

00:01:28.903 --> 00:01:32.399
a differentiable regularization for graphs: a
learnable permutation that lines nodes into

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:35.975
a single row while preserving permutation invariance,

00:01:36.055 --> 00:01:39.551
so Euclidean convolution finally applies.

00:01:39.631 --> 00:01:43.128
Second, the model built on it, the Compressed
Convolution Network, or CoCN.

00:01:43.208 --> 00:01:46.704
It learns both node and structure features and
trains end to end,

00:01:46.784 --> 00:01:50.280
beating competitive GNNs and pooling models.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:54.042
Here is how CoCN works.

00:01:54.122 --> 00:01:57.503
First,

00:01:57.583 --> 00:02:00.965
permutation generation regresses an absolute
position for each node with an MLP on Laplacian-smoothed features,

00:02:01.045 --> 00:02:04.426
so similar nodes land nearby.

00:02:04.506 --> 00:02:07.888
These form a relaxed permutation matrix,

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:11.349
controlled by a factor tau that provably converges
to a true permutation.

00:02:11.429 --> 00:02:14.811
With nodes in a row,

00:02:14.891 --> 00:02:18.272
CoCN slides a kernel along the diagonal of the
permuted edge and node matrices,

00:02:18.352 --> 00:02:21.734
extracting node features and their structure together.

00:02:21.814 --> 00:02:25.195
Compressed pooling builds a hierarchy,

00:02:25.275 --> 00:02:28.657
and transposed convolution up-samples for predictions.

00:02:28.737 --> 00:02:32.118
Crucially, this cuts permutation modeling from n
factorial to n squared,

00:02:32.198 --> 00:02:35.580
letting it scale to large graphs.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.743
The evaluation is broad.

00:02:37.823 --> 00:02:44.187
For graph classification, six datasets: MUTAG,
PROTEINS, NCI1, COLLAB, IMDB-Binary,

00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:47.913
and IMDB-Multi, under ten-fold cross-validation.

00:02:47.993 --> 00:02:54.356
For node classification, six heterophilic
benchmarks: Chameleon, Squirrel, Cornell,

00:02:54.436 --> 00:02:56.530
Texas, Wisconsin, and Actor.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:02.508
To rule out leakage they test filtered Chameleon
and Squirrel, and for scale,

00:03:02.588 --> 00:03:08.330
mini-batch experiments on three graphs with
hundreds of thousands of nodes.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.171
Results are strong across the board.

00:03:11.251 --> 00:03:16.747
On node classification, CoCN gets the best average
rank, one point eight three,

00:03:16.827 --> 00:03:21.618
topping Chameleon at seventy-nine percent, Squirrel
at seventy-three,

00:03:21.698 --> 00:03:23.453
and Cornell at eighty-six.

00:03:23.533 --> 00:03:27.265
On graph classification, it wins five of six datasets,

00:03:27.345 --> 00:03:29.523
including COLLAB and IMDB-Multi.

00:03:29.603 --> 00:03:34.041
And on filtered Chameleon and Squirrel, with
duplicates removed,

00:03:34.121 --> 00:03:38.770
it ranks first of eighteen models, so its edge is
not data leakage.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.676
Several ablations show why CoCN works.

00:03:42.756 --> 00:03:46.281
First, permutation heads matter: with more permutations,

00:03:46.361 --> 00:03:49.887
accuracy rises consistently on MUTAG, Chameleon,
Cornell, and Wisconsin,

00:03:49.967 --> 00:03:53.492
since each exposes different node arrangements.

00:03:53.572 --> 00:03:57.098
Second, larger diagonal kernels give a bigger
receptive field and better performance,

00:03:57.178 --> 00:04:00.703
without over-smoothing.

00:04:00.783 --> 00:04:04.309
Third,

00:04:04.389 --> 00:04:07.914
an information-loss study finds the relaxed
permutations preserve almost all information,

00:04:07.994 --> 00:04:11.520
so the regularization is lossless.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:17.395
CoCN reaches an average rank of one point eight
three across six node datasets,

00:04:17.475 --> 00:04:19.018
the best of any method.

00:04:19.098 --> 00:04:22.617
On Squirrel it scores nearly seventy-three percent,

00:04:22.697 --> 00:04:26.216
over eleven points above the strongest prior model.

00:04:26.296 --> 00:04:28.827
On COLLAB it hits eighty-six percent.

00:04:28.907 --> 00:04:34.120
And it cuts permutation modeling from intractable n
factorial to n squared.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.110
The takeaway is elegant.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:41.957
Learn a differentiable permutation that arranges a
graph's nodes into a single row,

00:04:42.037 --> 00:04:47.028
and all the machinery of image convolution becomes
available for graphs.

00:04:47.108 --> 00:04:51.113
CoCN does exactly this, provably convergent and invariant,

00:04:51.193 --> 00:04:53.508
reaching state-of-the-art results.

00:04:53.588 --> 00:04:57.100
Graphs, all in a row, can be convolved like images.
